<section id="organic-feed" class="about section paper-texture">
  <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up">
    <div class="row gy-4 align-items-center">
      <div class="col-lg-5 order-lg-2">
        <img src="/assets/img/9.11.26/ChickensInPasture.webp"
          class="img-fluid"
          alt="Young chickens at Smoky Mountain Acres">
      </div>
      <div class="col-lg-7 order-lg-1 content">
        <h2>Are Our Animals Fed Organic Feed?</h2>
        <p>
          Yes. When supplemental feed is needed, we choose organic feed appropriate for the species. Pasture, forage, and natural behaviors remain important parts of the system, but they do not replace the responsibility to meet an animal's nutritional needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          This describes the feed we choose. It is not a claim that our animals or farm products are USDA Certified Organic. USDA Organic certification is a specific regulatory designation with its own requirements and certification process, and we will use that term only if we hold the applicable certification.
        </p>
        <p>
          In plain language: <strong>pasture first, organic feed for supplementation, and no conventional feed as our standard feeding program.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="land-management" class="about section">
  <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up">
    <div class="row gy-4 align-items-center">
      <div class="col-lg-6">
        <img src="/assets/img/Smoky Mountain Acres/RoughPasture.webp"
          class="img-fluid"
          alt="Pasture at Smoky Mountain Acres in Cosby, Tennessee">
      </div>
      <div class="col-lg-6 content">
        <h2>How Do We Manage the Land?</h2>
        <p>
          We do not use synthetic fertilizers or pesticides on our farm. Instead, we focus on the slower work of building a useful pasture system: observing the land, managing animal pressure, encouraging forage, and allowing ground to rest.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><i class="bi bi-check2-all"></i> <span>Animals are moved between areas as pasture conditions and animal needs change.</span></li>
          <li><i class="bi bi-check2-all"></i> <span>Rested ground has time to recover after grazing, rooting, or heavy traffic.</span></li>
          <li><i class="bi bi-check2-all"></i> <span>Manure and animal movement become part of the fertility cycle instead of concentrating impact in one place.</span></li>
          <li><i class="bi bi-check2-all"></i> <span>We watch forage, weather, soil, and animal condition rather than follow a rigid calendar.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is a working system, not a promise that every field looks perfect. Healthy pasture can include grass, weeds, leaves, bare scratching spots, shade, and areas recovering from use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

